[FINAL-TEXT] I now have complete, direct evidence. Let me confirm the full picture:

- **R3 eth0**: `state DOWN`, IP 39.1.1.1/8, OSPF not enabled (because the interface is down)
- **R3 FRR config**: `network 39.0.0.0/8 area 0` IS present — the OSPF config is correct, but the daemon will not activate on a down interface
- **R9**: Fully healthy — all FRR daemons running, both interfaces up, OSPF running on eth0 (39.1.1.2/8) and trying to maintain the adjacency with 39.1.1.1 (R3) — but that adjacency's dead timer was at ~1.6 s and will expire repeatedly, causing the observed route flapping

The team's hypothesis (R9 hung) is **wrong**. R9 is operationally intact.

---

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r3
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies entirely on **R3**, not on R9. R3's eth0 interface (segment J, IP 39.1.1.1/8) is administratively/physically shut down — `ip link show eth0` reports `state DOWN` with no UP or LOWER_UP flags. This is the only link between R3 and R9 in the topology (segment J is shared exclusively by r3.eth0 and r9.eth0).

Because the interface is down, FRR's OSPF daemon on R3 cannot activate it, even though the correct network statement (`network 39.0.0.0/8 area 0`) exists in R3's running configuration. As a result, R3 sends no OSPF hellos toward R9. On R9's side, the OSPF dead timer for neighbor 39.1.1.1 (R3) expires every ~40 seconds, causing repeated neighbor-loss events. Each time the adjacency drops, R9's OSPF-advertised prefixes — the 19.x.x.x/24 loopbacks, 39.0.0.0/8, and 90.0.0.0/8 — are flushed from the LSDB and disappear from R1's RIB. The routes then briefly reappear when R9 re-elects itself DR on eth0, only to flap again, matching the instability reported in the ticket exactly.

R9 itself is completely healthy: all FRR daemons (zebra, ospfd, watchfrr) are running, both eth0 and eth1 are link-up, and OSPF is actively running on both interfaces.

**Root cause:** R3's eth0 interface was left in the `DOWN` state after the maintenance window, severing the only OSPF path to R9.

**Corrective fix:** On R3, run `ip link set eth0 up`. The existing OSPF network statement will immediately activate OSPF on eth0, the R3–R9 adjacency will re-form, and R9's prefixes will be re-advertised into the domain. No action on R9 is required.
=== END ===